#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180739 :tutorial/content "When dealing with sums or differences of square roots, we can combine any terms with the same algebraic expression under the square root symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom 0}.$ This is similar to collecting like terms in a linear expression.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n5\\sqrt{x} - 3\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{x}-3\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ (5-3)\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe *cannot* combine terms when the expressions under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n5\\sqrt{x} - 3\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt x$ and $\\sqrt y.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "deda60a8-4b35-50ac-a71c-d3f884aa8e1d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5971 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180749 :tutorial/content "Recall that the product rule for radicals states that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a\\cdot b} = \\sqrt{a}\\cdot \\sqrt{b}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are nonnegative.\n\nWe can use the product rule for radicals to simplify some expressions. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "30549c94-702e-5e00-8cdc-4a050a34243f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6012 :tutorial/title "The Product Rule for Radicals"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180750 :tutorial/content "We can simplify cube root expressions in the same way as with square roots.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt[3]x - 2\\sqrt[3] x\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt[3]{x}-2\\sqrt[3]{x} & = \\\\ (4-2)\\sqrt[3]{x} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt[3]{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs with square roots, we *cannot* combine terms when the expressions under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x-1} + \\sqrt[3]{x+1}\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt[3]{x-1}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{x+1}.$\n\nFinally, it is not possible to combine square root and cube root terms, even if the numbers under the radical symbol are the same! For example, the expression\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt x + \\sqrt[3] x\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt x$ and $\\sqrt[3] x.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a4a25921-e2bd-5bf7-99ed-3a8dd852fb70" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6013 :tutorial/title "Simplifying Expressions with Cube Roots"}]}
